<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 2.3 Taylor Series and its convergence.

  Covers 2.3.1-2.3.3 of the full lecture notes (Taylor.ptx).  The two
  remaining subsections of the original section - the applications
  (relativistic kinetic energy, the pendulum, approximating pi) and the
  proof of the remainder theorem - are deliberately NOT reproduced here;
  a pointer at the end of the section sends interested readers to the
  full notes.

  Theorems, definitions, and problem statements are kept; derivations
  and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes with a one-line
  instruction.  Every xml:id is prefixed with "skel-".

  Interactive figures (GeoGebra, Desmos) and the videos are marked
  component="web": publication.ptx keeps them, publication-print.ptx
  drops them, so they appear on the web but not in the PDF.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-series-taylor-convergence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Taylor Series and its convergence</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
The main objectives of this section are to answer the following questions:
</p>
</introduction>
<ol>
<li>
<p>
Under what conditions is the function <m>f(x)</m> equal to the Taylor
series generated by <m>f</m> at <m>x = a</m>? That is, when can we say
<m>f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!} (x-a)^n</m>?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
If the Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m>,
<m>p_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k</m>, is used as
an approximation to <m>f(x)</m>, how can we estimate the error in this
approximation? That is, how can we estimate the magnitude of
<m>R_n(x) = f(x) - p_n(x)</m>?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
<xref ref="thm-skel-taylor-formula"/> and
<xref ref="thm-skel-remainder-theorem"/> will enable us to answer these
questions. We begin by stating Taylor's formula and the remainder
estimation theorem, and then use them to bound the error in a Taylor
approximation, discuss some applications of these theorems, and close
with the proof of the remainder theorem.
</p>
<p>
<em>These are skeletal notes: the theorems and problem statements are
given, and blank boxes are left wherever a derivation or a solution
belongs, so that you can fill them in by hand.</em>
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-taylor-formula">
<title>Taylor's Formula and The Remainder Estimation Theorem</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-taylor-formula">
<title>Taylor's Formula</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f</m> be a function that has <m>n+1</m> continuous derivatives on
an open interval <m>I</m> containing <m>a</m>. Then for each
<m>x\in I</m> and for each positive integer <m>n</m>, there exists a
number <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that
<md xml:id="eq-skel-taylor-formula-eq" number="yes">
f(x) = p_n(x) + R_n(x),
</md>
where
<md xml:id="eq-skel-taylor-polynomial-eq" number="yes">
p_n(x)= \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k,
</md>
and
<md xml:id="eq-skel-taylor-remainder" number="yes">
R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-taylor-formula">
<caption>Taylor's Formula: <m>f(x) = p_n(x) + R_n(x)</m>. The Taylor
polynomial <m>p_n</m> agrees with <m>f</m> at <m>x = a</m>; away from
<m>a</m>, the vertical gap between them is the remainder
<m>R_n(x)</m>.</caption>
<image width="65%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-taylor-formula">
<diagram dimensions="(420,320)" margins="8">
<definition>a = 1</definition>
<definition>f(x) = 0.9 + 0.55*sin(1.15*(x - 0.4)) + 0.09*x</definition>
<definition>pn(x) = f(a) + 0.5778*(x - a) - 0.379*(x - a)^2</definition>
<definition>xt = 3.1</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.4, -0.3, 4.1, 2.3)">
<axes at="axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<graph at="fgraph" function="f" domain="(-0.2, 3.9)"
       stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
<graph at="pngraph" function="pn" domain="(-0.2, 3.9)"
       stroke="red" thickness="2"/>
<group at="tangency">
<point p="(a, f(a))" fill="black" size="3"/>
<line p1="(a, 0)" p2="(a, f(a))" dash="4 4" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
<label anchor="(a, 0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-2)"><m>a</m></label>
</group>
<group at="remainder">
<line p1="(xt, 0)" p2="(xt, f(xt))" dash="4 4" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
<label anchor="(xt, 0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-2)"><m>x</m></label>
<point p="(xt, f(xt))" fill="blue" size="3"/>
<point p="(xt, pn(xt))" fill="red" size="3"/>
<line p1="(xt, pn(xt))" p2="(xt, f(xt))"
      stroke="black" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
<label anchor="(xt, pn(xt))" alignment="sw" offset="(-8,-14)">
<m>R_n(x)</m>
</label>
</group>
<label at="flabel" anchor="(3.55, f(3.55))" alignment="n" offset="(0,8)">
<m>f(x)</m>
</label>
<label at="pnlabel" anchor="(3.5, pn(3.5))" alignment="e" offset="(6,-2)">
<m>p_n(x)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="fgraph"
    text="A schematic illustration of Taylor's Formula, f of x equals p n of x plus R n of x. A function f and its Taylor polynomial p n agree at x equals a and separate as x moves away, the gap being the remainder">
  <annotation ref="fgraph"
      text="The graph of the function f of x, shown in blue"
      sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="pngraph"
      text="The graph of the Taylor polynomial p n of x, shown in red, which is tangent to f at x equals a and closely matches f near a"
      sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="tangency"
      text="The point x equals a, where f and its Taylor polynomial p n agree; the approximation is exact here"/>
  <annotation ref="remainder"
      text="At a point x away from a, the vertical gap between f and p n is the remainder R n of x, the error in the Taylor approximation"/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p component="web">
For a visual representation of Taylor's formula, watch the animation in
<xref ref="fig-skel-vid-taylor-formula"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside component="web" width="90%">
<figure xml:id="fig-skel-vid-taylor-formula">
<caption>Taylor's Formula Animation.</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-taylor-formula"
       source="videos/taylor_formula_1080p.mp4"
