</figure>
</sidebyside>

<remark xml:id="rmk-skel-error-upper-bound">
<title>Finding an upper bound for the error term without knowing the value of <m>c</m></title>
<p>
Usually the value of <m>c</m> is not explicitly known. However, we may
manage to find <alert>an upper bound</alert> for the error term
<m>R_n(x)</m> without knowing the exact value of <m>c</m>. This is
achieved by finding an upper bound <m>M</m> for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m>,
where <m>c</m> is between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>, and then using this
upper bound to estimate the error term.
</p>
<p>
This is the idea behind every error estimate in this section. It is used
in <xref ref="subsec-skel-taylor-convergence"/> to prove that the Taylor
series of <m>e^x</m> converges to <m>e^x</m> for every <m>x</m>, and in
each of the three examples of
<xref ref="subsec-skel-taylor-error"/>.
</p>
</remark>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-remainder-theorem">
<title>The Remainder Estimation Theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f</m> be a function that has <m>n+1</m> continuous derivatives on
an open interval <m>I</m> containing <m>a</m>. Then for each
<m>x\in I</m> and for each positive integer <m>n</m>, there exists a
number <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that
<md xml:id="eq-skel-remainder-bound" number="yes">
|R_n(x)| \leq \frac{M}{(n+1)!} |x-a|^{n+1},
</md>
where <m>M</m> is an upper bound for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> on the
interval between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Derive <xref ref="eq-skel-remainder-bound"/> from
<xref ref="eq-skel-taylor-remainder"/>: take absolute values, then
replace <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> by its upper bound <m>M</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,4.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for deriving the remainder estimation theorem by hand.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
<alert>You are not expected to know the proof of Taylor's formula
itself</alert>, only to understand the statement and its implications.
The proof is worked through in <xref ref="subsec-skel-taylor-proof"/>.
</p>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-taylor-convergence">
<title>An example of a Taylor series that converges</title>

<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m>. Since <m>f^{(n)}(x) = e^x</m>
for all <m>n\geq 0</m>, the Taylor series generated by <m>f</m> at
<m>x=0</m> is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-exp-series" number="yes">
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!} x^n
= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n!} x^n,
</md>
and, putting <m>a = 0</m> and <m>f^{(n+1)}(c) = e^c</m> into
<xref ref="eq-skel-taylor-remainder"/>, its remainder is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-exp-remainder" number="yes">
R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1}
= \frac{e^c}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
We want to show that <xref ref="eq-skel-exp-series"/> converges to
<m>e^x</m> for every <m>x\in\R</m>, which happens exactly when
<m>R_n(x) \to 0</m>. Treat the two cases separately. In each one, follow
<xref ref="rmk-skel-error-upper-bound"/>: bound <m>e^c</m> using where
<m>c</m> must lie, then let <m>n\to\infty</m>. Read the bound on
<m>e^c</m> off <xref ref="fig-skel-exp-taylor"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$x &gt; 0$, so $0 \leq c \leq x$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {$x &lt; 0$, so $x \leq c \leq 0$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two halves, labelled x greater than zero and x less than zero, for bounding the remainder in each case.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
You will need the fact that
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} \dfrac{x^{n+1}}{(n+1)!} = 0</m> for every
fixed <m>x</m><fn>
Write the quotient as a product of <m>n+1</m> factors,
<m>\frac{|x|^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}
= \frac{|x|}{1}\cdot\frac{|x|}{2}\cdot\frac{|x|}{3}\cdots\frac{|x|}{n+1}</m>.
The numerators never change, but the denominators keep growing, so
eventually every new factor is small. Precisely, fix an integer <m>N</m>
with <m>N \geq 2|x|</m>. The first <m>N</m> factors contribute the fixed
number <m>C = \frac{|x|^{N}}{N!}</m>, and each factor after that
satisfies <m>\frac{|x|}{k} \leq \frac{1}{2}</m> for <m>k > N</m>. Hence
for <m>n+1 > N</m> we get
<m>0 \leq \frac{|x|^{n+1}}{(n+1)!} \leq C \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{n+1-N}</m>,
and the right-hand side tends to <m>0</m> as <m>n\to\infty</m>, so the
squeeze theorem gives the limit <m>0</m>. In short, the factorial in the
denominator eventually outgrows any fixed power in the numerator, no
matter how large <m>|x|</m> is.
</fn>.
</p>

   <figure xml:id="fig-skel-exp-taylor">
  <caption>Schematic graph of <m>f(x) = e^x</m> and its Taylor polynomials
  <m>p_1, \dots, p_4</m> about <m>0</m>. For a positive <m>x</m> the remainder
  uses some <m>c</m> with <m>0 &lt; c &lt; x</m>, giving <m>e^c &lt; e^x</m>;
  for a negative <m>x</m> it uses some <m>c</m> with <m>x &lt; c &lt; 0</m>,
  giving <m>e^c &lt; 1</m>.</caption>
  <image width="80%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-exp-taylor">
      <diagram dimensions="(440,360)" margins="8">
        <!-- Schematic: e^x with Taylor polynomials p1..p4 about 0.
             No axis numbers; dashed markers show a representative x>0 with an
             intermediate c (0<c<x), and a representative x<0 with c (x<c<0). -->
        <definition>f(x) = exp(x)</definition>
        <definition>p1(x) = 1 + x</definition>
        <definition>p2(x) = 1 + x + x^2/2</definition>
        <definition>p3(x) = 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6</definition>
        <definition>p4(x) = 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6 + x^4/24</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-2.7, -1.9, 2.25, 8)">
          <axes at="axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
          <graph at="p1" function="p1" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="p2" function="p2" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="green" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="p3" function="p3" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="purple" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="p4" function="p4" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
          <graph at="fgraph" function="f" domain="(-2.5, 2)" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
          <label at="flabel" anchor="(-1.55, 4.4)" alignment="w" color="blue">
