  </image>
</figure>

<p component="web">
The animation in <xref ref="fig-skel-exp-taylor-video"/> illustrates this
convergence geometrically: as <m>n</m> increases, the Taylor polynomials
<m>p_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{x^k}{k!}</m> hug the graph of
<m>f(x) = e^x</m> over a wider and wider interval, matching the fact that
<m>R_n(x)\to 0</m> for every <m>x\in\R</m>.
</p>
<p component="print">
An animation of this convergence <mdash/> the Taylor polynomials
<m>p_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{x^k}{k!}</m> hugging the graph of
<m>f(x) = e^x</m> over a wider and wider interval as <m>n</m> grows
<mdash/> accompanies the online version of these notes.
</p>

<sidebyside component="web" width="85%">
<figure xml:id="fig-skel-exp-taylor-video">
<caption>
The Taylor polynomials <m>p_1,\dots,p_8</m> of <m>e^x</m> about
<m>x=0</m> converging to <m>y=e^x</m>, followed by the
remainder-theorem argument.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-exp-taylor"
       source="videos/taylor_exp_convergence.mp4"
       width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</sidebyside>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-taylor-error">
<title>The error in using a Taylor polynomial</title>

<p>
Suppose we approximate <m>f(x)</m> by the Taylor polynomial
<xref ref="eq-skel-taylor-polynomial-eq"/> of degree <m>n</m> at
<m>x=a</m>. The error is <m>R_n(x) = f(x) - p_n(x)</m>, which is
<xref ref="eq-skel-taylor-formula-eq"/> rearranged, and is given by
<xref ref="eq-skel-taylor-remainder"/>. To bound it we need an upper
bound <m>M</m> for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> on the interval between
<m>a</m> and <m>x</m>, as in
<xref ref="rmk-skel-error-upper-bound"/>. The three examples below carry
out that step in three different settings.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-error-bound-exp">
<title>Approximating <m>e^x</m> using a Taylor polynomial</title>
<statement>
<p>
Approximate <m>e^{0.5}</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 at
<m>x=0</m>. Find an upper bound for the error in this approximation.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em> Write <m>p_2</m>, then bound <m>|R_2(0.5)|</m> using
<m>M = \max |f^{(3)}(c)|</m> for <m>c</m> between <m>0</m> and
<m>0.5</m>. Compare your bound with the actual error.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8) -- (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {$p_2(x) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {bound on $|R_2(0.5)|$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {actual error:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, labelled p sub 2 of x, bound on the remainder, and actual error.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p component="web">
<xref ref="fig-skel-interactive-error-exp"/> shows the two graphs and the
gap between them. The red curve is <m>f(x) = e^x</m> and the blue curve
is the Taylor polynomial <m>p_2(x)</m>; the vertical distance between the
red and blue points is the error at <m>x = 0.5</m>. Check that it is
smaller than the bound you found.
</p>

<sidebyside component="web" width="95%">
<figure xml:id="fig-skel-interactive-error-exp">
<caption>
<m>f(x) = e^x</m> in red, its Taylor polynomial <m>p_2(x)</m> in blue,
and the error at <m>x = 0.5</m> as the gap between the two points.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="xzdc5jpq" width="100%" aspect="16:11">
<description>
<p>
The red curve is the graph of <m>f(x) = e^x</m> and the blue curve is the
graph of the Taylor polynomial <m>p_2(x)</m>. The exact value of
<m>e^{0.5}</m> is represented by the red point and the approximation given
by <m>p_2(0.5)</m> is represented by the blue point. The vertical distance
between the two points represents the error in the approximation.
</p>
</description>
</interactive>
</figure>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-error-bound-sin-range">
<title>Given a desired error bound, find values of <m>x</m> for which the approximation is guaranteed to be valid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Assume that we use the Taylor polynomial of degree 3 at <m>x=0</m> to
approximate <m>\sin(x)</m>. For approximately what values of <m>x</m> can
you replace <m>\sin(x)</m> by such a Taylor polynomial with an error of
magnitude no greater than <m>4 \times 10^{-3}</m>?
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em> Note that <m>p_3 = p_4</m> here, so work with
<m>R_4</m>. Bound <m>|f^{(5)}(c)|</m>, then solve the resulting
inequality for <m>|x|</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$p_3(x) =$ \quad and \quad $R_4(x) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {bound on $|R_4(x)|$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {solve for $|x|$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the Taylor polynomial and remainder, the bound on the remainder, and solving for the range of x.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p component="web">
The two interactives in
<xref ref="fig-skel-interactive-sin-error"/> and
<xref ref="fig-skel-interactive-sin-range"/> let you vary <m>x</m> and
watch the error change, and confirm that it stays below
<m>4 \times 10^{-3}</m> on the range you found.
</p>

<sidebyside component="web" width="95%">
<figure xml:id="fig-skel-interactive-sin-error">
<caption>
<m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> in red and <m>p_3(x) = x - \frac{x^3}{6}</m> in
blue. Drag the slider to see how the error changes with <m>x</m>.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="ptgj7hpk" width="100%" aspect="16:10">
<description>
<p>
The red curve is the graph of <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> and the blue curve is
the graph of the Taylor polynomial <m>p_3(x)= x - \frac{x^3}{6}</m>. Use
the slider to change the value of <m>x</m> and see how the error changes
for different values of <m>x</m>. Note that the slider values have been
limited to fit the page.
</p>
</description>
</interactive>
</figure>
</sidebyside>

<sidebyside component="web" width="95%">
<figure xml:id="fig-skel-interactive-sin-range">
<caption>
The error as a function of <m>x_0</m>, staying below
<m>4 \times 10^{-3}</m> exactly on the range guaranteed by
<xref ref="thm-skel-remainder-theorem"/>.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="z854nrgd" width="100%" aspect="16:9">
<description>
<p>
Use the slider to change the value of <m>x_0</m> and see how the error
changes. Note that for <m>|x_0| \leq 0.86347</m>, the error is less than
